WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.150
Joining us right now is ocean explorer Tom
Detwiler, he led the team that found the wreckage

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:09.540
of the titanic, is also a close friend with
ph nargeolet one of the people on board the

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.269
missing vessel.

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:17.480
What are your new thoughts on this reporting
that we were talk being with Miguel Marquez

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:22.189
of search crews picking up repeated banging
sounds via sonar devices.

00:00:22.189 --> 00:00:24.180
Does that give you hope?

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:33.239
&gt;&gt; Well, like everybody, you want to hold
out hope until there can be no more hope.

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:38.460
The banging sounds are interesting because
knowing ph nargeolet and his experience with

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:44.590
submersibles and his background in submarines
this is exactly how he would try to indicate

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:48.469
that they were still alive and try to communicate
with surface.

00:00:48.469 --> 00:00:53.500
He would do some banging, do it on a neg lar
schedule so that it was dishable from the

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:54.500
background

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.500
[9:06:08 AM]

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.770
was dishable from the background noises that
are in the ocean all the time.

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:04.369
So it is encouraging that there did seem to
be a pattern to it and, you know, we are going

00:01:04.369 --> 00:01:06.460
to continue to hold out hope.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:07.460
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.580
Tom, is that -- do you think that that would
be -- you said that would be exactly what

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:12.580
he would be doing.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:18.540
Is that, do you think, ph would be improvising
or is this some sort of, I don't know, standard

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:26.280
protocol or procedure that you're familiar
of when it comes to underwater emergencies?

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:31.970
&gt;&gt; It is a standard protocol for contacting
any sort of rescue service that might be coming

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:33.870
your way.

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:36.990
&gt;&gt; That is super interesting.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.290
You've described ph as a close -- as a close
friend.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:45.390
I want to play for you, Tom, what another
ocean explorer David Gallo said about P.H.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.390
On CNN last night.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:48.039
Let me play this for you.

00:01:48.039 --> 00:01:50.079
&gt;&gt; If P.H.

00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:54.720
Nargeolet was sitting here with me like he
normally would be, this is what he would say

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.649
to do, let's just sit down

00:01:56.649 --> 00:01:57.649
[9:07:09 AM]

00:01:57.649 --> 00:01:59.259
say to do, let's just sit down together in
a small room, figure out what needs to be

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:01.790
done and let's make it happen, let's do it.

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:04.720
&gt;&gt; Tom, what do you think P.H.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.099
Would be saying right now?

00:02:07.099 --> 00:02:11.100
&gt;&gt; It would be exactly that.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:18.180
He is very calm, collected, expert in his
field and, you know, we would have approached

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:24.720
this in a very scientific manner, very straightforward
manner and just get the fastest reaction out

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:25.970
there that we could have.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:32.220
I've spent the last two days trying to get
an effort out there, it's on its weiwei now

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.520
and I know that P.H.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.580
Would have done the same thing for anybody
else out there, whether he knew them or not.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:43.280
The community would have drawn together just
like you've seen it over the last couple days

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.800
and we would do anything in our power to get
out there.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.640
Never questioning costs, never questioning
time, you know, just do it.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:53.750
Drop everything.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.849
&gt;&gt; Tom, talk to me about the effort and what
you're trying to

00:02:55.849 --> 00:02:56.849
[9:08:10 AM]

00:02:56.849 --> 00:02:57.849
effort and what you're trying to get out there
and what you've seen that's been deployed

00:02:57.849 --> 00:02:58.849
so far.

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:02.540
Talk to me about the scope and scale and what
they're also up against in terms of trying

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.439
to find them and get to them.

00:03:05.439 --> 00:03:11.830
&gt;&gt; Well, some of the experts have already
said that, you know, you see Navy assets going

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:16.170
out there, that's very important because the
Navy assets that are currently on their way

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:21.830
are the assets that are experienced in finding
things under water and doing recoveries from

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.140
very deepwater.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:29.530
Some of the few assets that are available
and they will get there within the next day

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:31.170
or so.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:35.670
That's the limitation is it's very far out
there and, you know, just getting this very

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:39.340
heavy equipment moving, get it to where it
needs to be, mobilizing it on the ship, as

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:45.270
you can imagine, when a ship goes out to sea
everything has to be fastened down so you're

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:49.810
doing a lot of welding and things like that
to make sure that it stays in place as the

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:55.060
ship starts to roll in the seas, and then,
you know, it has to be able to take the load

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:56.060
if you're doing a recovery.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:57.060
[9:09:11 AM]

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:58.060
doing a recovery.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:01.909
So there's a lot of work that has to be done,
but these are all guys who do this routinely

00:04:01.909 --> 00:04:10.610
and they do it quickly and I think that you
will see some success in finding it quickly.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:11.959
&gt;&gt; Where is your heart and head on this right
now?

00:04:11.959 --> 00:04:15.780
I know you said that you want to hold out
hope because you've got to hold out hope for

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:20.829
another day, you also know the realities of
the risks and the dangers down there.

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:26.380
I mean, you've -- you probably know it better
than anybody.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:30.770
&gt;&gt; My whole career has been in the very deep
ocean and I do know all the things that can

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:34.070
happen and I've run over all those in my head.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:41.910
But I've seen miracles happen at sea as well,
very unexpected things, very surprising things.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:45.440
So I'm going to continue to hold out hope.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.750
They still have some oxygen left and you have
to consider that the oxygen estimates are

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:52.400
based on normal activities in the sub.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:53.419
One thing P.H.

00:04:53.419 --> 00:04:57.699
Would have done is tried to calm everybody
down and keep them quiet so that

00:04:57.699 --> 00:04:58.699
[9:10:11 AM]

00:04:58.699 --> 00:04:59.699
and keep them quiet so that they're using
less oxygen.

00:04:59.699 --> 00:05:04.849
You can potentially extend that beyond the
estimates.

00:05:04.849 --> 00:05:11.460
&gt;&gt; Tom, I can't -- that's super important
to consider in all of this and thank you so

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:15.979
much for coming on, having your level of expertise
and also just to hear about your friendship

00:05:15.979 --> 00:05:16.979
with P.H.

00:05:16.979 --> 00:05:19.699
And what you know he would be doing down there
right now is really important for everybody.

00:05:19.699 --> 00:05:21.469
Thank you so much, Tom.

00:05:21.469 --> 00:05:24.539
&gt;&gt; You're quite welcome, Kate.

00:05:24.539 --> 00:05:25.539
&gt;&gt; John?

00:05:25.539 --> 00:05:28.950
&gt;&gt; That was a great discussion.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:33.040
&gt;&gt;&gt; This morning we have a firsthand account
of what it is like to take part in this kind

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:34.509
of underwater adventure.

00:05:34.509 --> 00:05:39.850
One year ago Simpson's writer Mike Reese was
on the same submersible that is missing in

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:40.850
the atlantic.

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:44.500
He told a CNN affiliate about the dangers
he proo prepared for ahead of the trip and

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:51.620
explained the navigational issues his team
experienced during the dive.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:59.601
&gt;&gt; It was just 11 months ago in this very
same sub and I see over and over on every

00:05:59.601 --> 00:06:00.601
dive we

00:06:00.601 --> 00:06:01.601
[9:11:15 AM]

00:06:01.601 --> 00:06:03.500
over and over on every dive we took they lost
communication.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:08.000
This is just one of the problems with this
thing.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:16.850
You know, I'm sure the world is very worried
and that kind of thing, but something to know

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:24.060
having gone through it is you know going in
how very dangerous this is.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:32.290
It's not any bad reflection on -- on Stockton
rush, it's his company and he designed it,

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:38.699
but it's just that we are all part of this
ongoing experiment.

00:06:38.699 --> 00:06:46.710
Before you even get on you sign this long,
long waiver that mentions possible death three

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:50.190
times on the first page.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:52.330
So you know what you're getting into.

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:58.940
In fact, when I stepped on to the sub I just
-- you know, part of my mind was going, well,

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:00.750
this could be the end.

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:01.750
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:02.750
[9:12:17 AM]

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:03.750
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:08.210
&gt;&gt; So that -- I mean, mostly, you know, it's
a very creepy feeling to think if they're

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:16.580
alive, down at the bottom of the ocean or
something like that and, you know, just knowing

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:22.319
I don't think there is a way to get them out
of the situation like that and that they will

00:07:22.319 --> 00:07:32.380
just be sitting there for three or four days
before the oxygen runs out, very scary thought.

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:33.380
&gt;&gt; Scary indeed.

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:38.700
I did note, Sara, he said that every dive
they took they lost communication.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.191
So people who are involved with

